//? Title: Fit a confirmatory factor model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Fit a specified loading pattern

Five synthetic measurements share one latent factor. Fix its variance to
one to set the scale. The model estimates all five loadings and each
measurement's residual variance. Fix the off-diagonal residual covariances
to zero to express the assumption that the factor accounts for all
covariation between measurements.

The example perturbs three entries of a covariance matrix to illustrate
imperfect fit. Gaussian maximum likelihood chooses the model-implied
covariance \(\Lambda\Phi\Lambda^\top+\Psi\). The observed covariance has
15 distinct entries and 10 fitted parameters, leaving 5 degrees of freedom.

```{cpp, id=cfa-loading-pattern, file="figures/cfa_loadings.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/confirmatory_factor_analysis.hpp>

#include <array>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    using dm::linalg::DenseMatrix;
    using Mask = DenseMatrix<std::uint8_t>;

    const DenseMatrix<double> signal(5, 1, {.75, .62, .83, .51, .67});
    auto observed = signal * signal.transpose();
    const std::array noise{.50, .42, .30, .66, .55};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < noise.size(); ++i)
        observed(i, i) += noise[i];
    observed(0, 1) = observed(1, 0) += .13;
    observed(2, 3) = observed(3, 2) -= .08;
    observed(3, 4) = observed(4, 3) += .07;

    const dm::stats::CFASpecification model{
        {DenseMatrix<double>(5, 1, .55), Mask(5, 1, 1)},
        {DenseMatrix<double>(1, 1, 1), {}},
        {.6 * DenseMatrix<double>::identity(5), Mask::identity(5)}};
    const auto fit = dm::stats::fit_confirmatory_factor_analysis(observed, model);
    if (fit.status != dm::stats::CFAStatus::Converged)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected convergence for the example covariance");

    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(55, 760);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle latent;
    latent.circle = true;
    latent.width = latent.height = 1.5;
    latent.fill = {224, 239, 252};
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle indicator;
    indicator.width = 1.65;
    indicator.height = .7;
    diagram.node("factor", {5, 3.7}, "Factor\nVar = 1", latent);

    // Orient the factor so the first loading is positive for display.
    const double orientation = fit.loadings(0, 0) < 0 ? -1 : 1;
    std::println("| Measurement | Loading | Residual variance |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < noise.size(); ++i) {
        const auto id = std::format("X{}", i + 1);
        const double loading = orientation * fit.loadings(i, 0);
        diagram.node(id, {2.5 * i, 0}, id, indicator)
            .edge("factor", id, std::format("{:.2f}", loading));
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", id, loading, fit.residual_covariance(i, i));
    }
    std::println("\nThe discrepancy per observation is {:.5f} with {} degrees of freedom.",
        fit.discrepancy, fit.degrees_of_freedom);
    std::println("\nThe observed X1–X2 covariance is {:.3f} and the fitted covariance is {:.3f}.",
        observed(0, 1), fit.implied_covariance(0, 1));
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    diagram.save_svg("build/cfa_loadings.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measurement | Loading | Residual variance |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| X1 | 0.820 | 0.390 |
| X2 | 0.683 | 0.337 |
| X3 | 0.768 | 0.398 |
| X4 | 0.483 | 0.687 |
| X5 | 0.661 | 0.562 |

The discrepancy per observation is 0.06073 with 5 degrees of freedom.

The observed X1–X2 covariance is 0.595 and the fitted covariance is 0.560.
\image(figures/cfa_loadings.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Arrows show the fitted loadings from the common factor to the five measurements.)
\alttext(A factor with variance one points to X1 through X5, with loadings 0.82, 0.68, 0.77, 0.48, and 0.66.)

The positive X1–X2 covariance residual shows where the single-factor
model underestimates the observed association. Reversing the signs of
all five loadings gives the same implied covariance, so the displayed
factor is oriented to make the X1 loading positive.

[Jöreskog (1969)|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289343]
